# Didi @VERSION@ for @PLATFORM_NAME@

Didi (`godot-mcp-native`) is a native Model Context Protocol server for the
Godot editor, version 4.5 or newer. It comes in two halves that are installed
in two different places: a server your AI client starts, and an editor plugin
that goes into your Godot project.

| Path | What it is |
| :--- | :--- |
| `bin/@EXE@` | The MCP server. Your client runs this. |
| `bin/didi_class_reference.json` | The Godot class reference the server reads offline. Keep it beside the server. |
| `addons/didi/` | The Godot editor plugin, with its native extension in `addons/didi/bin/@LIBRARY@`. |
| `LICENSE` | Didi's license (MIT). |
| `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt` | The licenses of the third-party code compiled into both binaries. |

## Install

<!-- platform:macos -->
**Clear the download quarantine first.** These binaries are signed ad hoc and
not notarized, so macOS refuses to run the server or load the library when a
browser downloaded them. Run this once on the extracted folder, before you copy
anything out of it:

```
xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine /path/to/didi-macos-arm64
```

<!-- /platform:macos -->
1. Put this folder somewhere permanent, **outside** any Godot project. The
   server stays here and your client is pointed at it.
2. Copy `addons/didi` into your Godot project, so that it sits at
   `res://addons/didi`. Copy the whole folder: the plugin loads every file in
   it.
3. Open the project in Godot, go to **Project > Project Settings > Plugins**,
   and enable **Didi Native MCP Bridge**. A **Didi** tab appears beside 2D, 3D
   and Script.
4. In the Didi tab, open **Connect** and set the server path to `bin/@EXE@` in
   this folder. It is stored in Editor Settings as
   `didi/server/executable_path`. The console never looks for the server
   inside a project, so it will not find it on its own unless `bin` is on your
   `PATH`.
5. Still in **Connect**, pick your client and copy the configuration it
   generates. It already has the server path and your project's path filled
   in. The **Dashboard** page shows a card per part, green when that part is
   working.

Configuring a client by hand looks like this, with your own two paths:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "didi": {
      "command": "@EXAMPLE_COMMAND@",
      "args": ["--project", "@EXAMPLE_PROJECT@"]
    }
  }
}
```

## Platform notes

<!-- platform:windows -->
- x64 only.
- The C++ runtime is built into both binaries, so no Visual C++
  Redistributable is needed.
<!-- /platform:windows -->
<!-- platform:macos -->
- Apple silicon (arm64) only, macOS 14 or newer. There is no Intel build. On
  an Intel Mac, Godot reports that no library exists for the machine rather
  than loading one it cannot run.
- If you copied `addons/didi` into a project before clearing the quarantine,
  clear it on the copy as well:
  `xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine /path/to/project/addons/didi`
<!-- /platform:macos -->
<!-- platform:linux -->
- x86_64, glibc 2.34 or newer: Ubuntu 22.04, Debian 12, Rocky, RHEL and
  AlmaLinux 9, or anything newer.
- The C++ runtime is linked statically, so glibc is the only system library
  version that matters.
<!-- /platform:linux -->

## Upgrading

Replace the server and the addon together, and close Godot first so the
extension library is not in use. The two halves report a build id, and the
Didi tab shows the bridge amber when they come from different builds.

## Verifying this download

The release page carries `SHA256SUMS` and a signed build provenance
attestation for every archive. With the GitHub CLI installed:

```
gh attestation verify @ARCHIVE_FILE@ --repo saworbit/didi --signer-workflow saworbit/didi/.github/workflows/release.yml --source-ref refs/tags/v@VERSION@
```

